KS-SPMD, the RC5 ISPC Core
==========================

This is Sunset's quick and dirty core for the Intel SPMD Program Compiler,
ISPC. It draws heavily from the ANSI cores.

What's ISPC?
------------

ISPC is Intel's compiler and language, based on C with some conservative
extensions. It enables a more GPU-style programming model for SIMD programming.

Why should I care?
------------------

KS-SPMD has, in my testing, significantly outperformed ANSI cores. The first use
is to get us AVX512 support essentially for free, but we should be able to
target any platform or instruction set supported by ISPC; I've tested on ARM64
in addition to x86 (although ARM64 KS-SPMD performance did not exceed the MONIKA
assembly core.) If Intel comes out with AVX1024 in a year, we can support it as
soon as ISPC does.

How does it perform?
-------------------- 

Per my testing, KS-SPMD performs below hand-tuned cores at iso width. On the same
hardware (Xeon Gold 6126):

  YK-AVX2: 58mn key/s
  KS-SPMD AVX2: 35mn key/s
  KS-SPMD AVX512: 95mn key/s

In the absence of a hand-written AVX512 assembly core, KS-SPMD is the fastest
core available on AVX512-capable hardware.

What hardware does it support?
------------------------------

KS-SPMD has been tested on "Knights Landing" Xeon Phi and "Skylake-SP" Xeon
hardware. It should run on "Cannon Lake", "Ice Lake", "Cascade Lake", and any
other AVX512-capable hardware with a minimum of KNL or SKL compatibility.

Can it be made faster?
----------------------

Almost certainly; this was written as a proof-of-concept that took an unusually
practical turn. The C++ harness is derived almost entirely from the ANSI cores
and result-checking, especially, could be made faster.
